 For ischemic priapism lasting at least 36 hours, non-surgical management is associated with a high retreatment burden. In a 2026 retrospective single-center cohort of 76 patients (38 non-surgical, 38 surgical), reported readmission or reintervention rates were 66.7% with non-surgical care versus 10.4% with surgical care (p &lt; 0.01).<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 
-**Predicting who will need a shunt.** Two retrospective cohorts identify factors associated with progression to surgical shunting after failed medical therapy. In a series of 169 ischemic-priapism encounters, 74% related to intracavernosal injections, duration (odds ratio 1.05 per hour) and prior priapism (odds ratio 3.15) predicted shunting; a 24-hour cutoff had 77% sensitivity and 90% specificity in this cohort and may not generalize to other etiologies.<sup>[[50]](#ref50)</sup> In a separate cohort of 147 patients presenting with a first episode of ischemic priapism, a total phenylephrine dose above 950 mcg or an erection duration beyond 15.5 hours were each associated with a significantly higher likelihood of requiring corporoglanular shunting.<sup>[[51]](#ref51)</sup> These thresholds can help set expectations for escalation to the operating room rather than repeating ineffective bedside aspiration and phenylephrine.
+**Predicting who will need a shunt.** Two retrospective cohorts identify factors associated with progression to surgical shunting after failed medical therapy. In a series of 169 ischemic-priapism encounters, 74% related to intracavernosal injections, duration (odds ratio 1.05 per hour) and prior priapism (odds ratio 3.15) predicted shunting; a 24-hour cutoff had 77% sensitivity and 90% specificity in this cohort.<sup>[[50]](#ref50)</sup> In a separate cohort of 147 patients presenting with a first episode of ischemic priapism, a total phenylephrine dose above 950 mcg or an erection duration beyond 15.5 hours were each associated with a significantly higher likelihood of requiring corporoglanular shunting.<sup>[[51]](#ref51)</sup> These thresholds can help set expectations for escalation to the operating room rather than repeating ineffective bedside aspiration and phenylephrine.
 
