-**Complications.** The defining cavernovenous-shunt risk, persistent ("permanent open") shunt with venous-leak ED, applies to Barry as much as to Grayhack. Kulmala documented this in 19.2% of post-shunt-impotence patients with 100% impotence reversal on closure, and Moloney recommended graft ligation if erection had not returned by 3 months.<sup>[[28]](#ref28)[[31]](#ref31)</sup> Micoogullari's ten-patient series used planned shunt closure at two months; 8/10 maintained erectile function after a median priapism duration of 13.5 hours.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup> The uncontrolled series cannot show whether planned closure caused that outcome or be directly compared with the heterogeneous proximal-shunt cohorts.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[15]](#ref15)</sup> Barry-specific reports document no urethrocutaneous fistulae, no saphenous donor-site morbidity (original technique), and no recurrence in the Micoogullari and Chiou series.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)[[33]](#ref33)[[34]](#ref34)</sup>
+**Complications.** The defining cavernovenous-shunt risk, persistent ("permanent open") shunt with venous-leak ED, applies to Barry as much as to Grayhack. Kulmala documented this in 19.2% of post-shunt-impotence patients with 100% impotence reversal on closure, and Moloney recommended graft ligation if erection had not returned by 3 months.<sup>[[28]](#ref28)[[31]](#ref31)</sup> Micoogullari's ten-patient series used planned shunt closure at two months; 8/10 maintained erectile function after a median priapism duration of 13.5 hours.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)[[1]](#ref1)[[15]](#ref15)</sup> Barry-specific reports document no urethrocutaneous fistulae, no saphenous donor-site morbidity (original technique), and no recurrence in the Micoogullari and Chiou series.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)[[33]](#ref33)[[34]](#ref34)</sup>
 
 **Current status and algorithmic placement.** The EAU review based its conclusion that proximal or venous shunts should be abandoned on series of several proximal-shunt types (209 patients; potency 11.1–77.2% across studies).<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> The Barry-shunt series are small and uncontrolled (Chiou: 16 patients, priapism resolved or improved in all, and erections in 9/13 [69%] adults with ischemic priapism followed for up to 6.5 years; Micoogullari: 10 patients, detumescence in all and EF maintained in 8/10 [80%]).<sup>[[34]](#ref34)</sup><sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup> Micoogullari suggests the Barry shunt could be considered as a first treatment for refractory low-flow priapism. AUA/SMSNA 2022 includes Barry shunts in its proximal-shunt evidence discussion but does not endorse Barry as a first surgical treatment.<sup>[[53]](#ref53)</sup> It recommends counseling about uncertain benefit after distal shunt failure (statement 12; Moderate Recommendation, Grade C); the small Barry series do not replace that pathway.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[15]](#ref15)[[34]](#ref34)[[53]](#ref53)</sup>
 
